result.concern_2_hash_canonicalization_collisio:
BLOCKER -- CONFIRMED, empirically. compute_hash/2's canonicalization joins sorted, deduped capability strings with a raw 0x0A (LF) byte and no escaping, and does not forbid capability strings from containing 0x0A themselves (validate_shape/1 only checks `is_binary`). This makes two GENUINELY DIFFERENT, non-duplicate capability sets collide onto the identical hash whenever one capability string itself contains an embedded LF that reproduces the same byte sequence as a delimiter-joined split of it into multiple entries. Verified directly (ran as a temporary ExUnit test against the real compute_hash/2, output captured, scratch test file since deleted -- not part of the diff):

  m1 = %Manifest{script_id: "s", capabilities: ["a\nb", "c"]}
  m2 = %Manifest{script_id: "s", capabilities: ["a", "b", "c"]}
  Manifest.compute_hash(m1, "return 1") == Manifest.compute_hash(m2, "return 1")  # => true (both '3e27beb2b211f3dc3cc054dd4b9cedaecdcf3fea813bb54be6e471508c4cf425')

These are not the intended dedup case ({'a','a','b'} vs {'a','b'}) -- ['a\nb','c'] and ['a','b','c'] are semantically distinct capability declarations (one grants a single odd token 'a\nb' plus 'c'; the other grants three separate capabilities 'a', 'b', 'c') that must never share a hash under LUA-07's own acceptance text ('modified manifest without re-registration is rejected'). This defeats the core guarantee: a manifest could be edited (e.g. splitting or merging capability grants across an embedded LF) without changing the registered hash, so validate_at_load/3 and LuaScriptAudit.verify_manifest_hash/2 would both silently accept a materially different capability set. I also checked the script_id/capability-list 0x00 boundary the design's moduledoc specifically discusses (manifest.ex:315-323's footnote, claiming 0x00 'cannot appear inside a String.t() ... in practice'): script_id='ab'+cap=['c'] vs script_id='a'+cap=['bc'] did NOT collide (0x00 separator held in this specific pair), but the design's underlying premise is still wrong as stated -- Elixir String.t()/binary() values CAN contain a literal 0x00 byte (NUL is valid UTF-8 codepoint 0); nothing in validate_shape/1 forbids it. The LF-based capability-list collision above is sufficient on its own to fail this invariant; the NUL-boundary claim is flagged as a second, related latent risk that should be closed by the same fix rather than left resting on an incorrect 'cannot appear in practice' assumption.
